---
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
import { loadDataset } from "../lib/data";
import { SUM_TOLERANCE } from "../lib/schema";

const { predictions, meta, isMock } = loadDataset();
const pipe = meta.pipeline;
const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-GB");
const fieldSizes = [...new Set(predictions.map((p) => p.drivers.length))].sort((a, b) => a - b);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="What Sector 4 predicts, which data it is built on, how each prediction is scored against the result, and what the model cannot see."
  image="/og/methodology.png"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Methodology", path: "/methodology/" }]}
>
  <section class="band band-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How the numbers are made</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        A prediction you cannot check is a claim, not a forecast. Everything below is here so the
        numbers on this site can be argued with.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What the model is, and what it is not</h2>
      <p>
        The model is a logistic regression with <strong>four inputs</strong>: where the driver
        qualified, their best practice lap relative to the fastest car, their championship points,
        and their share of their team's points. Qualifying position enters as a smooth curve rather
        than a straight line, because the gap between first and second is not the gap between
        fifteenth and sixteenth.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four inputs is not a simplification made for the sake of the explanation. Larger versions
        were built and measured — nineteen features, twenty-two, thirty-three — and every one of
        them scored worse. Circuit history, tyre data and driver-against-teammate form were all
        tried, and all three made the predictions less accurate rather than more.
      </p>
      <p class="flag">
        <strong>The model has not been shown to beat a simple baseline.</strong> The baseline is
        qualifying order: the historical chance of winning from each grid slot, and nothing else.
        Across 2019–2025 the model scored better, but that comparison is what chose the model, so it
        flatters it. The one clean out-of-sample test — the 2026 season, held back from every
        decision and scored once — was <strong>inconclusive</strong>. The model was ahead on
        average, and well inside the range that fourteen races of a regulation-reset season cannot
        separate from chance.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this site does not claim that the model beats qualifying order. What it does claim is
        that the probabilities are well calibrated — when it says 20%, that happens about one time
        in five — and <a href="/track-record/">the track record</a> prints the baseline's score
        beside the model's on every race, so the question is yours to settle rather than ours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {isMock && (
    <section class="band band-soft">
      <div class="wrap prose">
        <h2>What is running right now</h2>
        <p class="flag">
          <strong>The numbers on these pages are sample data, not the model's output.</strong> The
          model described above exists and has been measured, but this site is still showing
          probabilities generated to exercise the layout — a Plackett–Luce draw over a plausible
          field, scored against invented results. They say nothing about any real race.
        </p>
        <p>
          Real predictions are being written one race at a time, each committed before the session
          it predicts and scored afterwards in a separate step, so the record cannot be edited once
          the result is known. This page will lose this notice when those reach the site. The banner
          at the top of every page and the label on every link preview come from the same flag, so
          neither can be forgotten.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The data</h2>
      <p>
        Results, qualifying, grids, laps, pit stops and status codes come from the Ergast schema:
        the Kaggle Formula 1 World Championship export for 1950 to 2024, and the Jolpica F1 API for
        2025 onward. Session telemetry, timing and weather from 2018 onward come from FastF1.
      </p>
      <p>Five rules govern that pipeline, and they are enforced rather than intended.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing is invented.</strong> A missing value stays missing and is flagged. No
          interpolation, no back-filling, no plausible substitutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Provenance travels with the row.</strong> Every record carries the source it came
          from, so any figure can be traced back to the file it was read from.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Conflicts are logged, not resolved quietly.</strong> Where two sources disagree,
          both values are recorded with the evidence, and the resolution is a documented decision
          rather than a silent preference.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Time is UTC.</strong> Stored in UTC, rendered in your zone by the page. Original
          local date fields are kept as they were.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Builds are deterministic.</strong> The same inputs produce byte-identical outputs,
          which is what makes a change reviewable at all.
        </li>
      </ul>
      {pipe && (
        <Fragment>
          <h3 class="sub-h">What that adds up to</h3>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table>
              <caption class="sr-only">
                Row counts per table, split by the source each row came from.
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Table</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Rows
                  </th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Kaggle
                  </th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Jolpica
                  </th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {Object.entries(pipe.tables).map(([name, t]) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{name.replace(/_/g, " ")}</th>
                    <td class="n num">{fmt.format(t.rows)}</td>
                    <td class="n num dim">{fmt.format(t.by_source.kaggle ?? 0)}</td>
                    <td class="n num dim">{fmt.format(t.by_source.jolpica ?? 0)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <dl class="summary">
            <div>
              <dt>Seasons covered</dt>
              <dd class="num">
                {pipe.seasons.first}–{pipe.seasons.last}
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Validation checks</dt>
              <dd class="num">
                {pipe.validation.passed ?? "—"} passed, {pipe.validation.failed ?? "—"} failed
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Corrections applied</dt>
              <dd class="num">{pipe.corrections.total}</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Unresolved conflicts</dt>
              <dd class="num">{pipe.unresolved_conflicts}</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            Of those corrections,{" "}
            <span class="num">{pipe.corrections.by_evidence.official ?? 0}</span> cite an official
            source — an FIA classification document or a formula1.com results page — and{" "}
            <span class="num">{pipe.corrections.by_evidence.internal ?? 0}</span> cite an internal
            derivation, where the dataset contradicts itself and the resolution follows from other
            rows rather than from an outside document. Both kinds are recorded with what changed and
            why. Session timing from <span class="num">{pipe.enrichment_first_season}</span> onward
            is layered on top from FastF1, and is used to fit the model but never published — see{" "}
            <a href="/credits/">credits</a>.
          </p>
        </Fragment>
      )}
      <p>
        The current data version is <span class="num">{meta.data_version}</span>. Sources and
        licences are listed on <a href="/credits/">the credits page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Two snapshots per race</h2>
      <p>
        A prediction is only meaningful with a timestamp on it, so each race gets up to two, and
        neither is ever revised after the fact.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <dt>Pre-weekend</dt>
        <dd>
          Published before any car runs. It sees season form, car pace, circuit history and
          reliability, and knows nothing about the grid.
        </dd>
        <dt>Post-qualifying</dt>
        <dd>
          Published after qualifying, with the grid known, including penalties. This is the sharper
          of the two and the one a race page shows by default.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Both remain on the race page afterwards. Comparing them is the honest way to see how much of
        the prediction was the grid and how much was everything else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What is predicted</h2>
      <p>For every driver in the field, each snapshot gives:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Win, podium, top ten</strong> — the probability of finishing there or better.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>DNF</strong> — the probability of not being classified as a finisher.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Expected position</strong> — the mean of the driver's full finishing-position
          distribution, not a rounded guess at a place.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Top factors</strong> — the handful of inputs moving that driver's number most,
          with direction and relative weight.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Those numbers have to hold together, so the build checks them rather than trusting them.
        Across a field of {fieldSizes.join(" or ")} drivers, win probabilities must sum to one,
        podium probabilities to three and top-ten probabilities to ten; each driver's position
        distribution must sum to one; and no driver may be likelier to win than to podium, or
        likelier to podium than to finish in the top ten. The tolerance is{" "}
        <span class="num">{SUM_TOLERANCE.toExponential(0)}</span>, which is float noise and nothing
        more. A file that fails any of these stops the build instead of reaching a page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>How a prediction is scored</h2>
      <p>
        Scoring happens once, against the snapshot that was public before the race started, and the
        result is never recomputed. Four numbers per race:
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <dt>Log loss</dt>
        <dd>
          <span class="num">−ln p</span>, where <span class="num">p</span> is the probability given
          to the driver who actually won. It punishes confident misses hard, which is the point: a
          model that says 60% and is wrong should hurt more than one that says 20% and is wrong.
          Lower is better.
        </dd>
        <dt>Brier score</dt>
        <dd>
          The squared error of the whole win distribution, summed over every driver, with one for
          the winner and zero for everybody else. Unlike log loss it is bounded, so a single
          catastrophic race cannot dominate a season. Lower is better.
        </dd>
        <dt>Winner called</dt>
        <dd>
          Whether the driver given the highest win probability won. Crude, and included because it
          is the number everyone actually checks.
        </dd>
        <dt>Podium places</dt>
        <dd>
          How many of the three drivers given the highest podium probability finished on the podium,
          out of three.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        A log loss means nothing without a reference, so{" "}
        <a href="/track-record/">the track record</a>
        prints two of them: what a uniform random pick over the same field would have scored, which
        is the floor, and what qualifying order scored, which is the bar. Both are computed the same
        way as the model's own number, on the same race, from data available before it started.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What was tried and rejected</h2>
      <p>
        The four features are not a first guess that happened to work. Larger and cleverer models
        were built, measured against the same baseline on the same races, and found worse.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <dt>Nine features that measured as noise</dt>
        <dd>
          Circuit history, tyre data and driver-against-teammate form — nine columns across three
          groups. Not one of them differed from zero at 95% confidence, and adding them moved the
          log loss the wrong way: 1.3286 with form alone, 1.3480 once circuit history was added,
          1.3765 once tyre data was.
        </dd>
        <dt>A gradient-boosted tree model</dt>
        <dd>
          It memorised its training set — 0.0069 log loss on data it had seen against 0.2155 on data
          it had not. Regularising it heavily closed that gap to 0.0256 and it still scored worse
          than the simple model at race level.
        </dd>
        <dt>An Elo rating</dt>
        <dd>
          2.0256 against the baseline's 1.4340. A rating that sees only the driver cannot see the
          car, and in this sport the car is most of the answer.
        </dd>
        <dt>A ranking model with simulated race orders</dt>
        <dd>
          It produced a coherent joint distribution, which the four marginals do not, but it lost to
          the baseline by a margin that did not depend on chance. Its simulation still supplies the
          position distributions shown on race pages.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>How the model is evaluated</h2>
      <p>
        Each race is predicted by a model fitted on races strictly before it and on nothing else.
        That is slow — the model is refitted for every race — and it is the only way the comparison
        against a baseline that works the same way means anything.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <dt>Bootstrap intervals, resampled over races</dt>
        <dd>
          Not over driver rows. Twenty drivers in one race are not twenty pieces of evidence: if the
          model misreads the car, it misreads all twenty at once.
        </dd>
        <dt>Paired against the baseline</dt>
        <dd>Race by race, so a run of unusually hard races cannot decide the comparison.</dd>
        <dt>Calibration beside discrimination</dt>
        <dd>
          Discrimination asks whether the ordering is right. Calibration asks whether a stated 20%
          happens about one time in five. This site publishes the number itself, so the second
          question is the one that matters, and a model with a better log loss and worse calibration
          would be the wrong choice here.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="flag">
        The evidence that the model beats the baseline is <strong>in-sample</strong>. The comparison
        across 2019–2025 is what chose this model, so it flatters it. The one clean test — 2026,
        held back from every decision and scored once — was inconclusive.{" "}
        <a href="/track-record/">The track record</a> is the real ongoing test, and it starts empty.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Two bugs in the scorer, and how they were caught</h2>
      <p>
        Both were found by the cheapest thing in the project, and both would have quietly flattered
        every number measured afterwards.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <dt>A uniform guess scored a 99.4% hit rate</dt>
        <dd>
          The field arrives sorted by finishing position, so picking the highest probability fell
          back on row order and handed a tied predictor the winner every time. Breaking ties by
          driver id instead gave 19.9% — still wrong, because the lowest id in this era belongs to a
          driver who won a fifth of those races. Ties are now settled by expectation, and a uniform
          guess scores exactly one in twenty, which is arithmetic rather than judgement.
        </dd>
        <dt>A ranking model gave the actual winner zero</dt>
        <dd>
          It scored five times worse than a random guess, assigning a near-certainty to one driver
          in most races, because its confidence was tuned on the same races it had learned from.
          Every accuracy metric looked healthy while this happened — the winner hit rate sat at 52%.
          Only the probabilities showed it.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What this cannot predict</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Safety cars and first-lap incidents.</strong> A large part of what separates the
          prediction from the result, and irreducible rather than a gap to be closed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Weather on the day.</strong> A dry forecast that turns wet rearranges a race, and
          a probability published on a Tuesday cannot know that.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reliability.</strong> Modelled as a rate, which is the right shape for a season
          and useless for a particular car on a particular afternoon.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anything not in the data.</strong> Team orders, an undisclosed upgrade, a driver
          racing unwell.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There is also one feature deliberately missing. <strong>Grid position</strong> is strictly
        better information than qualifying position because it includes penalties, and it is
        knowable before the race starts — but there is no feed that would let the model use it live
        as well as in training. Training on it and serving something else would make every score on
        this site an overstatement, so it stays out until that feed exists.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .sub {
    color: var(--colour-on-dark-soft);
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 26px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-base);
  }
  .prose p,
  .prose li {
    color: var(--colour-body);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-base);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .prose strong {
    color: var(--colour-ink);
  }
  .prose ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-base);
  }
  .prose li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .defs {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-base);
  }
  .defs dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--colour-ink);
    margin-top: var(--space-base);
  }
  .defs dd {
    margin: var(--space-xxs) 0 0;
    color: var(--colour-body);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .sub-h {
    font-size: 18px;
    margin: var(--space-xl) 0 var(--space-base);
  }
  .scroller {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 420px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-xs);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  thead th {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--colour-muted);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--colour-hairline);
  }
  tbody tr {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--colour-hairline);
  }
  tbody th {
    color: var(--colour-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .n {
    text-align: right;
  }
  .dim {
    color: var(--colour-muted);
  }
  .summary {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-base);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-lg);
  }
  .summary div {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--colour-hairline);
    padding-top: var(--space-xs);
  }
  .summary dt {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--colour-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .summary dd {
    margin: var(--space-xxs) 0 0;
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--colour-ink);
  }
  .flag {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--colour-caution);
    background: var(--colour-caution-surface);
    padding: var(--space-base) var(--space-lg);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
  }
</style>
